someone that they might, of course they're going to say no. It's not compelling. It's not an invasive scenario, it doesn't subvert or challenge one's conventional understandings. People <i>know</i> to say No to that. They know to say No to the guy trying to buy you.<br /> <br /> If there is an appeal to make, It is not through blatant displays of force, it's through subtle and insidious manipulations of that which a person takes pride in. The Seers aren't going to put a gun to your head, they're going to find ways to make you question yourself, what you stand for. They will find the cracks in your armor and they will take advantage of that with velvet gloves. Sure, they'll keep up exterior pressure, but they aren't going to compromise the entire's organization as the soft second place.<br /> <br /> If the Seers try to win someone over, the first thing they are always going to do is get someone to <i>give up</i>. Completely on their own accord.<br /> <br /> And, you know, it's hard for anyone to know just <i>how</i> they would give up. The worlds we live in seem very sure to us-they are designed that way, and we are programmed to make it easy to find, because otherwise we'd be gibbering balls of neuroses on overload. These things seem safe and solid to us, and it takes a lot to make them ephemeral. If you told me five years ago that I would be planning on leaving the Mormon Church, I'd have laughed at you. It now seems inevitable to me, but then, it was inconceivable, and that's with me having been aware of a majority of the things that did, in fact, go and play into why I finally got tired of the bullshit.<br /> <br /> And this is half of why my point is <i>you won't know until you're there</i>, because it's inconceivable to see that which is firm around us fade away into smoke. This is why I generally do not posit why Kumiko specifically would. It's a personal affair.<br /> <br /> (The other half of the point is that saying &quot;i wouldn't&quot; basically reads to